---
import CaseStudy from "../../layouts/CaseStudy.astro";
import Divider from "../../components/Divider.astro";
import Figure from "../../components/Figure.astro";

const metrics = [
  { value: "$309.77", label: "Average shipping cost" },
  { value: "$188.36", label: "Average order value" },
  { value: "164.5%", label: "Break-even conversion rate" },
  { value: "3.22%", label: "Actual conversion rate" },
];

const links = [
  { label: "Repository", href: "https://github.com/minminchudu/ecommerce-recommendation-margin-analysis" },
  { label: "Decision deck", href: "/media/Linh_Le_IterLight_Launch_Decision.html" },
  { label: "Dashboard (PDF)", href: "/media/Linh_Le_IterLight_PowerBI_Dashboard.pdf" },
  { label: "Power BI file", href: "/media/Linh_Le_IterLight_Dashboard.pbix" },
];
---

<CaseStudy
  title="Break-even required a 164.5% conversion rate"
  description="An e-commerce recommendation engine was lifting conversion and eroding margin at the same time, by working exactly as designed. IterLight Lab case study by Linh (Min) Le."
  project="IterLight Lab"
  period="March to June 2026"
  tint="code"
  hook="A recommendation engine was lifting conversion and eroding gross margin at the same time, and it was doing both by working exactly as designed."
  metrics={metrics}
  links={links}
>


    <section class="section">
      <div class="shell">
        <div class="prose">
          <h2 class="h-sub">The decision this informed</h2>
          <p>
            The work centred on an e-commerce recommendation and logistics system modelled on
            Wayfair. Its ranking formula scores every item as predicted conversion probability
            minus alpha times predicted shipping cost, where alpha decides how heavily an
            expensive-to-ship item gets pushed down the ranking. Three alpha settings were
            running against a baseline cohort, and the decision on the table was which one
            to launch.
          </p>
          <p>
            Sitting behind that question was an argument about tuning. Conversion had been climbing
            while gross margin fell, and the prevailing explanation held that alpha was set too low,
            letting the engine promote items that sold well and shipped expensively. The launch
            decision was the deliverable. The answer that came back changed what the question was.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="shell">
        <div class="prose">
          <h2 class="h-sub">The finding</h2>
          <p>
            The diagnosis ran across thirteen months of platform history. Over that window the
            platform spent an average of
            <strong>$309.77</strong> shipping an order and collected an average of
            <strong>$188.36</strong> when one converted. Every completed sale opened a
            <strong>$121.41</strong> hole. Reaching break-even on those economics would have
            required a conversion rate of <strong>164.5%</strong>, fifty-one times the
            <strong>3.22%</strong> the platform was actually running, which is a mathematician&rsquo;s
            way of saying the arithmetic had no solution. Rising conversion made the position worse,
            because each additional sale realised the loss one more time.
          </p>
          <p>
            Alpha was never going to close that gap. The formula governs which items appear and in
            what order, and it has no reach into what an item costs to ship or what a customer pays
            for it. Establishing that reframed every remaining week as loss reduction rather than a
            route to profitability, and it moved the real fix to carrier contracts and product
            pricing, which are procurement and Board decisions rather than algorithmic ones.
          </p>
          <Figure
            src="/figures/iterlight-service-level-economics.png"
            alt="Chart of margin per impression and cost decomposition across the GROUND, 2DAY and OVERNIGHT shipping tiers, showing linehaul and last-mile costs flat across tiers while the service premium rises steeply."
            caption="Cost decomposition by service tier. Linehaul and last-mile costs barely move between tiers. The entire gap between GROUND and OVERNIGHT is service premium, which makes it a policy the business chose rather than a distance it has to pay for."
          />
          <p>
            Within the narrower range alpha does control, the experiment returned a null under its
            own pre-registered rules with a clear direction underneath it. Setting alpha to
            <strong>0.25</strong> was the only arm that moved gross margin the right way, holding at
            <strong>+$0.63 per impression</strong> once warehouse-delay contamination was removed,
            <strong>+$0.98</strong> raw and <strong>+$2.19</strong> trimmed. At a million impressions
            a month that direction is worth roughly <strong>$7.5M a year</strong> against a system
            losing <strong>$4.24M a month</strong>, which is the baseline locked from the full
            500,000-record set before the experiment opened. It still did not launch. Its conversion rate
            landed at <strong>2.991%</strong>, beneath the <strong>3.3146%</strong> floor written
            into the protocol before any outcome data was opened, and the experiment carried one
            twelfth of the sample needed to confirm a signal that small.
          </p>
          <p>
            The recommendation was no-go, with the blocking constraint named to a single number and
            the follow-on sized exactly: two arms, 89,000 impressions each, roughly three months,
            against <strong>$1.88M</strong> in forgone upside while it runs.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="shell"><Divider /></div>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="shell">
        <div class="prose">
          <h2 class="h-sub">What I did, and the obvious alternative I rejected</h2>
          <p>
            The primary metric was gross margin per impression, with non-conversions entered as zero.
            Conversion rate is the reflexive choice for a recommendation experiment, and choosing it
            would have rigged the test against the mechanism under examination, because raising alpha
            works precisely by accepting fewer conversions in exchange for cheaper ones. A metric that
            penalises the intended effect cannot adjudicate it. Gross margin per impression was the
            only measure that moved with both effects at once, and the
            <strong>r = &minus;0.711</strong> correlation between category conversion rate and category
            margin, found earlier in the project, is the evidence that the two dimensions genuinely
            pull against each other.
          </p>
          <Figure
            src="/figures/iterlight-cvr-margin-anomaly.png"
            alt="Bubble chart of margin per impression against conversion rate for twelve furniture categories. Bubble size is impressions and colour is average order value. A downward trend line marked r equals minus 0.71 runs from Lighting at the top left to TV Stands at the bottom right."
            caption="The core anomaly. Every category that converts well loses more money, at r = −0.71. Bubble size is how many impressions the algorithm allocated, which shows it spending most heavily on the worst end of the line."
            wide
          />
          <p>
            The protocol carried three guardrails where five had been sketched. Five guardrails across
            three arms produce a <strong>54%</strong> probability of blocking a sound result by chance,
            which would have made the safety framework itself the most likely cause of a failed launch.
            Each surviving guardrail was tied to a specific failure mode already documented in the
            diagnostic phase, and each threshold was derived rather than chosen. The conversion floor
            came from the Wilson confidence interval lower bound on the baseline cohort.
          </p>
          <p>
            Everything arguable after the fact was committed in writing before outcome data was opened.
            The metric, the guardrail thresholds and their derivations, the four conditions a winner had
            to satisfy simultaneously, and a rule constraining how a null result could later be interpreted.
          </p>
          <p>
            Before any of that, the shipping cost model had to be worth trusting. The supplied predicted
            cost column correlated with actual cost at <strong>r = 0.9999</strong>, a figure no genuine
            forecasting model reaches and a reliable signature of a value computed after the shipment
            rather than before it. Chi-square and logistic tests across variant, conversion outcome,
            origin region and service level returned nothing significant, which supported treating the
            <strong>4.68%</strong> of missing values as missing completely at random and imputing them
            from actual cost behind a transparency flag.
 Three distinct leakage routes came out of the
            feature set, and the price of removing them is visible in the numbers. A model retaining the
            proxy scored <strong>RMSE $41.33</strong>; the clean 93-feature production model scored
            <strong>$61.55</strong> with <strong>R&sup2; 0.9024</strong>. The version that looked worse
            on paper was the only one that would survive live traffic.
          </p>
          <Figure
            src="/figures/iterlight-residuals-by-segment.png"
            alt="Chart of shipping cost model residuals broken out by segment, showing systematic under-prediction concentrated in specific regions and periods."
            caption="Residuals by segment. The 32% under-prediction tail is not spread evenly. It sits almost entirely in the long-haul zone and the holiday peak, which is the distribution the training window never saw."
          />
          <p>
            Warehouse delays hit the West Coast between November 1 and 15, inside the test window, and
            landed unevenly across arms. Difference-in-differences was the textbook correction, and it
            failed its own robustness check, shifting up to <strong>$2.20 per impression</strong> across
            reasonable definitions of the delay window against a pre-registered tolerance of
            <strong>$0.50</strong>. A mechanistic estimate put the delay&rsquo;s actual cost at
            <strong>$9,686 a month</strong> for the worst-affected arm, comfortably under the
            <strong>$42,379</strong> escalation threshold. Restricting the comparison to non-delayed
            rows preserved the arm ordering across every robustness approach and became the estimate
            of record, with the DiD result reported beside it as indeterminate.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="shell">
        <header class="section-head">
          <p class="eyebrow">Evidence</p>
          <h2 class="h-sub">The executive dashboard</h2>
          <p class="section-note">
            Built in Power BI for the diagnostic phase and used by leadership to see where the
            margin was going. The working file is downloadable below.
          </p>
        </header>
        <Figure
          src="/figures/iterlight-powerbi-page1.png"
          alt="Power BI executive dashboard showing margin per impression of negative 4.51 dollars, average shipping cost of 309.77 dollars, average order value of 188.36 dollars and a 3.22 percent conversion rate, alongside charts covering the conversion-margin anomaly, service level economics and seasonal cost movement."
          caption="Every headline on this dashboard states a finding instead of naming a metric. Higher conversion does not mean better margin. OVERNIGHT converts best and loses 35 times more than GROUND. Shipping cost rises 17% in peak season, exactly when conversion peaks."
          wide
        />
        <p class="embed-note">
          <a href="/media/Linh_Le_IterLight_PowerBI_Dashboard.pdf">Both pages as PDF</a>
          &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp;
          <a href="/media/Linh_Le_IterLight_Dashboard.pbix">Download the Power BI file</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="shell">
        <header class="section-head">
          <p class="eyebrow">Evidence</p>
          <h2 class="h-sub">The launch decision, as delivered</h2>
          <p class="section-note">
            Nine slides translating seven weeks of analysis into a decision, built as a
            self-contained page. Open it full width if the frame is tight.
          </p>
        </header>
        <div class="embed">
          <iframe
            src="/media/Linh_Le_IterLight_Launch_Decision.html"
            title="IterLight Lab launch decision deck"
            loading="lazy"
          ></iframe>
        </div>
        <p class="embed-note">
          <a href="/media/Linh_Le_IterLight_Launch_Decision.html">Open the deck in its own tab</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="shell"><Divider /></div>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="shell">
        <div class="prose">
          <h2 class="h-sub">Assumptions and limitations</h2>
          <p>
            Gross margin here is order value minus shipping cost. It excludes cost of goods and
            platform overhead, so the true loss is deeper than every figure above.
          </p>
          <p>
            The A/B assignment was pre-existing rather than randomised for this study. A sample ratio
            mismatch test returned <strong>&chi;&sup2; = 59.04</strong>, diagnosed as cohort-design
            imbalance with a 100% activity rate, which leaves the arm estimates unbiased while removing
            any claim to a clean randomisation.
          </p>
          <p>
            Sample size is the binding limitation and it cannot be fixed after the fact. The baseline
            cohort holds 5,041 in-window impressions, and because every comparison draws on that same
            pool, the harmonic effective n is <strong>7,471</strong> against the <strong>33,268</strong>
            the conversion target required and the <strong>89,000</strong> that confirming the
            alpha-0.25 signal would need. The null result is what a genuine $0.63 effect looks like at
            one twelfth of the necessary power, and it should not be read as evidence that alpha does
            nothing.
          </p>
          <p>
            The cost model has a <strong>32%</strong> under-prediction tail, concentrated almost
            entirely in November and December. December 2025 contributed zero training rows, so the
            model was extrapolating into a distribution it had never observed. Non-peak months sit at
            an 8 to 10 percent noise floor.
          </p>
          <p>
            Two findings are exploratory and are labelled that way. The East region result, at
            <strong>+$4.89 per impression</strong> and p = 0.009, does not survive Bonferroni
            correction across the sixteen segment tests run alongside it. The <strong>$1.2M a month</strong>
            estimated from adding an order-value term to the scoring formula is observational, assumes
            user conversion behaviour holds constant, and needs its own experiment before anyone spends
            against it.
          </p>
          <p>
            Service level accounts for <strong>66%</strong> of the cost model&rsquo;s feature importance,
            which makes deployment conditional on that field being available at scoring time. Without it
            the production model degrades to RMSE $189.09.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

</CaseStudy>
